    <ferc:DerivativeInstrumentAssets id="f-813" contextRef="c-105" unitRef="u-02" decimals="0">5788793</ferc:DerivativeInstrumentAssets>
    <ferc:LongTermPortionOfDerivativeInstrumentLiabilities id="f-815" contextRef="c-105" unitRef="u-02" xsi:nil="true" />
    <ferc:Donations id="f-817" contextRef="c-77" unitRef="u-02" decimals="0">500000</ferc:Donations>
    <ferc:BalanceTransferredFromIncome id="f-819" contextRef="c-77" unitRef="u-02" decimals="0">18399212</ferc:BalanceTransferredFromIncome>
    <ferc:AppropriatedRetainedEarningsIncludingReserveAmortization id="f-820" contextRef="c-105" unitRef="u-02" decimals="0">0</ferc:AppropriatedRetainedEarningsIncludingReserveAmortization>
    <ferc:AppropriatedRetainedEarningsIncludingReserveAmortization id="f-821" contextRef="c-79" unitRef="u-02" decimals="0">0</ferc:AppropriatedRetainedEarningsIncludingReserveAmortization>
    <ferc:NotesToFinancialStatementsTextBlock id="f-823" contextRef="c-77">&lt;div&gt;&lt;p&gt;1.  Regulatory Reporting and Presentation&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The accompanying financial statements are prepared in accordance with the accounting requirements of the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (&amp;quot;FERC&amp;quot;), as set forth in its applicable Uniform System of Accounts and published accounting releases and orders received by DCR Transmission, L.L.C. (the &amp;quot;Company&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;DCRT&amp;quot;, 'we&amp;quot;, &amp;quot;our&amp;quot;, or &amp;quot;us&amp;quot;), which is a comprehensive basis of accounting other than generally accepted accounting principles in the United States of America, (&amp;quot; US GAAP&amp;quot;). The accompanying financial statements are prepared considering specific FERC reporting requirements and orders received by DCRT.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;2.  Business and Organization&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;DCRT was formed on August 13, 2015, to develop, construct and operate an approximately 125-mile long, 500 kV transmission line (further referred to as &amp;quot;Ten West Link&amp;quot; or &amp;quot;TWL&amp;quot;) between the Delaney Substation in Arizona and the Colorado River Substation in California. The Company was a development stage company until the commercial operation date on June 12, 2024 (the &amp;quot;Commercial Operation Date&amp;quot;), and, as such, had no operations through June 11, 2024. The Company is wholly owned by DCR Holdings, LLC (&amp;quot;HoldCo&amp;quot;), which is wholly owned by DCR Transmission Holdings, LLC (&amp;quot;Parent&amp;quot;).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;On September 15, 2015 (&amp;quot;219 Effective Date&amp;quot;), pursuant to Section 219 of the Federal Power Act (the &amp;quot;FPA&amp;quot;), DCRT requested authorization from the Federal Energy Regulatory Commission (&amp;quot;FERC&amp;quot;) for certain incentive rate treatments for TWL to incentivize the Company to pursue the development, permitting, financing, construction, and operation of TWL. More specifically, DCRT requested that FERC authorize the Company to establish a regulatory asset for (A) all prudently incurred pre-commercial costs with respect to TWL, and (B) a carrying charge on such prudently incurred pre-commercial costs. In addition, the Company requested approval to seek recovery of 100% of any prudently incurred costs in the event TWL must be abandoned for reasons outside of the Company's reasonable control. The Commission approved the Company's request on December 17, 2015 (the &amp;quot;Approved Order&amp;quot;).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The development of DCRT is indirectly funded by Parent, which was also formed on August 13, 2015. At the time of its formation, Parent was indirectly owned by DCR Investor, LLC (&amp;quot;DCR Investor&amp;quot;). On October 29, 2015 (&amp;quot;Inception&amp;quot;), the limited liability company agreement of Parent was amended and restated to bring in an additional member and start funding the Company and the development of TWL. At Inception, DCR Investor owned 75% of Parent and another noncontrolling member owned the other 25%. On February 28, 2017, the noncontrolling member sold its investment and DCR Investor increased its ownership to 87.5%. The other 12.5% interest in Parent was acquired by ABY Holdings USA, LLC.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;3.  Summary of Significant Accounting Policies&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Use of Estimates&lt;br /&gt;The preparation of financial statements in conformity with FERC accounting requirements requires management to make estimates and assumptions that affect the reported amounts of assets and liabilities and disclosure of contingent assets and liabilities, at the date of the financial statements and the reported amounts of revenues and expenses during the reporting period. Significant estimates and assumptions may include, but are not limited to, the useful lives of long-lived assets, property taxes, and valuation of asset retirement obligation and all derivative financial instruments. Actual results could differ from those estimates.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Regulatory Accounting&lt;br /&gt;Pursuant to the Approved Project Sponsor Agreement (&amp;quot;APSA&amp;quot;) executed between DCRT and the California Independent System Operator Corporation (&amp;quot;CAISO&amp;quot;), DCRT is a Participating Transmission Owner (&amp;quot;PTO&amp;quot;), a role which was formalized upon commercial operation of Ten West Link through the Transmission Access Charge. As of the Commercial Operation Date, DCRT is subject to the regulatory jurisdiction of FERC, which issues orders pertaining to rates and recovery of certain costs. The electric transmission operations of the Company meet the US GAAP accounting standard that recognizes the FERC-regulated cost-based rate setting process, which could result in differences in the application of US GAAP. The FERC-regulated ratemaking process results in the recording of regulatory assets or regulatory liabilities based on the probability of current and future estimated cash flows. Regulatory assets represent incurred or accrued costs that have been deferred because they are probable of future recovery from customers. Regulatory liabilities may represent collections from customers that have been deferred because they will be expended in the future, or they may relate to the future cost of removal of assets.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Cash and Cash Equivalents&lt;br /&gt;The Company considers all highly liquid investments purchased with an original maturity of three months or less to be cash equivalents. The carrying amount of these instruments approximates fair value because of their short-term maturity.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Property, Plant and Equipment&lt;br /&gt;Property, plant, and equipment is recorded at cost and is being depreciated over an estimated life between 36 and 60 years using the straight-line method. FERC reporting requirements prescribe that the historical accumulated depreciation associated with the assets be maintained and reported in the Company general ledger system. The estimated group life was based on average service life estimates of peer electric transmission plants. Depreciation expense was $11,175,869 and $6,496,661 for the year ended December 31, 2025 and the period from June 12, 2024 through December 31, 2024, respectively, and is included in Depreciation, amortization, and accretion on the Statement of Operations. There was no depreciation expense prior to June 12, 2024. All new property and equipment is being depreciated on a straight-line basis over the estimated useful lives of the respective assets. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, transmission plant accumulated depreciation amounted to $17,672,529 and $6,496,661, respectively.&lt;br /&gt;The Company evaluates its long-lived assets for impairment as events or changes in circumstances indicate the carrying value of these assets may not be fully recoverable. If the undiscounted future cash flows before interest associated with the expected uses and eventual disposition of those assets are not greater than the carrying value of those assets, the Company would record an impairment loss based on the fair value of the asset. There was no impairment of long-lived assets as of December 31, 2025.&lt;br /&gt;The Company has identified certain electric transmission assets that will not contribute to future cash flow due to regulatory directives. These assets, while not impaired under the applicable accounting guidance, are considered unrecoverable for ratemaking or cost recovery purposes. As such, they are excluded from the Company's rate base and are not expected to be recovered through future revenues. These unrecoverable assets remain in service and continue to provide operational support to the transmission network. The Company will continue to depreciate these assets over their remaining useful lives. The net book value of these unrecoverable but non-impaired assets was $7,419,631 and $4,946,723 as of December 31, 2025 and 2024, respectively.&lt;br /&gt;The Company continues to monitor these assets and will reassess their recoverability and impairment status as conditions warrant.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Construction Work in Progress and Regulatory Asset&lt;br /&gt;Project development costs, which include permitting, preconstruction, construction, and administrative costs, directly attributable to the Company, have been capitalized as Construction work in progress on the Balance Sheet until the Commercial Operation Date. Once operational, such amounts are depreciated over future periods consistent with the period of expected recovery authorized by FERC. As of December 31, 2024, project development costs in relation to two Large Generator Interconnection Agreements (each an &amp;quot;LGIA&amp;quot;) obligating the Company to build switchyards connecting solar facilities to the grid through Ten West Link (see Note 8) were capitalized as Construction work in progress on the Balance Sheet. On January 31, 2025, one of the switchyards became commercially operational. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, project development costs totaling $20,552,897 and $97,369,696, respectively shows as capitalized Construction work in progress on the Balance Sheet. On January 31, 2025, one of the switchyards became commercially operational with $90,873,035 being capitalized out of construction work in progress balance of $97,369,696 in 2024.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Accounts Receivable and Revenue Recognition &lt;br /&gt;The Company recognizes revenue throughout the year based on its Transmission Revenue Requirement (TRR), which sets a fixed annual revenue target, ensuring predictable income regardless of actual usage. This aligns with the Company's performance obligations to maintain and operate transmission infrastructure for reliable electricity delivery to load-serving entities. Under the contract with the California Independent System Operator (CAISO), customers are billed based on actual usage, calculated using the TRR and estimated load. The performance obligation is satisfied by providing continuous access to the transmission network, with revenue recognized over time as this service is delivered. Any discrepancies between billed amounts and the TRR are managed through the Transmission Revenue Balancing Account (TRBA), recorded as regulatory liabilities or assets, ensuring proper handling of over- or under-recovery in line with regulatory requirements. This method ensures the financial statements accurately reflect the Company's operations and commitments.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, accounts receivable consists primarily of amounts owed to the Company from California Independent System Operator (CAISO), with a balance of approximately $7,131,324 and $7,061,449, respectively. If the customer receivable balance should be deemed uncollectible, it could have a material effect on the Company's results of operations and financial condition. However, management does not believe significant credit risk exists as of December 31, 2025 and 2024. Accounts receivable balances would be adjusted for any write-offs and any estimated allowance for credit losses, which is reviewed periodically based on amounts past due and significance. There was no allowance for credit losses recorded as of December 31, 2025 and 2024.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Other (Expense) Income&lt;br /&gt;As a FERC regulated utility, DCRT accrues AFUDC on capital spending for rate-regulated projects for which a return on such costs is recovered after the project is placed in service. The debt component of AFUDC is credited to Interest expense and the equity component is credited to Other income on the Statements of Operations. For the years ended December 31, 2025 and 2024, the Company capitalized and recorded AFUDC within Property, plant and equipment and Regulatory assets on the Balance Sheets and corresponding Other income (expense) totaling $1,405,887 and $13,404,014, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Income Taxes&lt;br /&gt;Given the Company's status as a FERC-regulated public utility, the Company accounts for income taxes using the accrual method. This practice requires the recognition of deferred tax assets and liabilities for the expected future tax consequences attributable to differences between the financial statement carrying amounts of existing assets and liabilities and their respective tax bases. Deferred tax assets, representing future tax benefits, are recognized to the extent that their realization is more likely than not to occur.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The deferred tax provision or benefit represents the change in the deferred tax assets and liabilities from the prior year. Although the Company is a single member LLC disregarded for US tax purposes, management has elected to record a tax provision on a separate return basis as the company's results are included in the corporate tax return of its owner.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Valuation allowances are established when necessary to reduce deferred tax assets to the amount that is more likely than not to be realized. The Company considers the future taxable income and ongoing tax planning strategies in assessing the need for a valuation allowance. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, management determined no valuation allowances were necessary. Deferred taxes presented in these statements are prepared on a separate return basis as the Company is a single member LLC.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Company accounts for uncertainty in income taxes in accordance with guidance issued by the Financial Accounting Standards Board (&amp;quot;FASB&amp;quot;). A tax position is recognized as a benefit only if it is &amp;quot;more likely than not&amp;quot; that the tax position would be sustained in a tax examination, with a tax examination being presumed to occur. The amount recognized is the largest amount that is greater than 50% likely of being realized on examination. For tax positions not meeting the &amp;quot;more likely than not&amp;quot; test, no tax benefit is recorded. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, there were no uncertain tax positions.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Fair Value Measurements&lt;br /&gt;DCRT utilizes valuation techniques that maximize the use of observable inputs and minimize the use of unobservable inputs, to the extent possible. DCRT determines fair value based on assumptions that market participants would use in pricing an asset or liability in the principal or most advantageous market. When considering market participant assumptions in fair value measurements, the fair value hierarchy distinguishes between observable and unobservable inputs. As of December 31, 2025 and 2024, the carrying values of cash and cash equivalents, accounts payable and accrued expenses, approximate fair value due to the short-term maturities of these instruments.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;DCRT applies the US GAAP accounting provisions related to fair value measurements. These provisions define fair value as the price that would be received to sell an asset or paid to transfer a liability in an orderly transaction between market participants at the measurement date, establish a hierarchy that prioritizes the information used in developing fair value estimates and require disclosure of fair value measurements by level within the fair value hierarchy. The hierarchy gives the highest priority to quoted prices in active markets (Level 1 measurements) and the lowest priority to unobservable data (Level 3 measurements), such as the reporting entity's own data. These provisions also provide valuation techniques, such as the market approach (comparable market prices), the income approach (present value of future income or cash flows) and the cost approach (cost to replace the service capacity of an asset or replacement cost).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A financial instrument's categorization within the valuation hierarchy is based upon the lowest level of input that is significant to the fair value measurement. The three levels of valuation hierarchy are defined as follows:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Level 1  Observable inputs such as quoted prices (unadjusted) for identical assets or liabilities in active markets.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Level 2  Inputs other than quoted prices for similar assets and liabilities in active markets, and inputs that are observable for the asset or liability, either directly or indirectly, for substantially the full term of the financial instrument.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Level 3   Unobservable inputs that reflect the Company's own assumptions.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The methods described above may produce a fair value calculation that may not be indicative of net realizable value or reflective of future fair values. Furthermore, although the valuation methods are determined to be appropriate and consistent within the industry, the use of different methodologies or assumptions to determine the fair value of certain assets and liabilities could result in a different estimate of fair value at the reporting date.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;DCRT measures its financial instruments at fair value. The following table presents the fair value and hierarchy levels for the financial instruments as of December 31, 2025 and 2024:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;table border="0" cellspacing="0" cellpadding="0" width="588" style="margin-left: 36.1pt" class="e-rte-paste-table"&gt;  &lt;tr style="height: 11.55pt"&gt;   &lt;td width="180" valign="top" style="width: 134.65pt; padding: 0; height: 11.55pt"&gt;   &lt;p style="margin: 0; font-size: 11pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Arial&amp;quot;, sans-serif"&gt;&lt;span style="font-size: 10pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Times New Roman&amp;quot;, serif"&gt;&amp;#160;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;   &lt;/td&gt;   &lt;td width="12" valign="top" style="width: 9pt; padding: 0; height: 11.55pt"&gt;   &lt;p align="center" style="margin: 0; font-size: 11pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Arial&amp;quot;, sans-serif; tex